From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wine.inf: a new user's DWM colour settings

Windows writes the DWM colour settings for every profile (HKCU\Software\Microsoft\
Windows\DWM: AccentColor, ColorizationColor, ColorizationAfterglow and their balances,
ColorPrevalence, EnableAeroPeek, Composition); Wine wrote none until Settings saved an
accent, so programs reading them found nothing (MusicBrainz Picard: 'Failed reading
ColorizationColor from registry', it used its own colour). wine.inf writes them,
only where unset, with the default Stained Glass uses everywhere else (Settings,
dwmapi, uxtheme: purple 7B2FBE; ColorizationColor c47b2fbe as Settings writes it).
Shell parts that fell back to BlueViolet 8A2BE2 when no accent was set (accent icons,
the desktop pager) now get the same purple as the rest.

Gate: test/dwmdefaults-gate.sh (inf data, no mutant: it fails on 10.0-1151).
---
--- a/loader/wine.inf.in
+++ b/loader/wine.inf.in
@@ -865,6 +865,16 @@
 HKCU,Software\Microsoft\SystemCertificates\Trust,,16
 HKCU,Software\Microsoft\SystemCertificates\TrustedPeople,,16
 HKCU,Software\Microsoft\SystemCertificates\TrustedPublisher,,16
+HKCU,Software\Microsoft\Windows\DWM,"AccentColor",0x10003,0xffbe2f7b
+HKCU,Software\Microsoft\Windows\DWM,"ColorizationColor",0x10003,0xc47b2fbe
+HKCU,Software\Microsoft\Windows\DWM,"ColorizationAfterglow",0x10003,0xc47b2fbe
+HKCU,Software\Microsoft\Windows\DWM,"ColorizationColorBalance",0x10003,0x59
+HKCU,Software\Microsoft\Windows\DWM,"ColorizationAfterglowBalance",0x10003,0x0a
+HKCU,Software\Microsoft\Windows\DWM,"ColorizationBlurBalance",0x10003,0x01
+HKCU,Software\Microsoft\Windows\DWM,"ColorizationGlassAttribute",0x10003,0x01
+HKCU,Software\Microsoft\Windows\DWM,"ColorPrevalence",0x10003,0
+HKCU,Software\Microsoft\Windows\DWM,"EnableAeroPeek",0x10003,1
+HKCU,Software\Microsoft\Windows\DWM,"Composition",0x10003,1
 HKLM,Software\Microsoft\TermServLicensing,,16
 HKLM,Software\Microsoft\Transaction Server,,16
 HKLM,Software\Microsoft\WBEM,"Installation Directory",2,"%11%\wbem"
